#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:1
msgid "Compiler Optimizations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:3
msgid ""
"cuda-oxide already benefits from optimizations in rustc and LLVM. The `mir-"
"transforms` crate is where cuda-oxide adds its own passes while a program is "
"still represented as `dialect-mir`. Loop unrolling is the first cuda-oxide-"
"owned optimization pass in this layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:8
msgid ""
"This page starts with the user-facing idea, then follows one loop through "
"the compiler. You do not need compiler experience to read it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:13
msgid "What is a MIR transform?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:15
msgid ""
"After rustc lowers Rust source to MIR, cuda-oxide imports it into `dialect-"
"mir`, where typed operations and control-flow branches are explicit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:18
msgid "There are three related jobs in this part of the compiler:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "Job"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "What it does"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "**Analysis**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "Reads the IR and records facts, such as which blocks form a loop"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "**Transform**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "Rewrites the IR without changing what the program computes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "**Lowering**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:0
msgid "Converts the program from one IR dialect into a lower-level dialect"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:26
msgid ""
"Loop analysis can identify a loop's counter, step, and bound. The unroll "
"transform uses those facts to rewrite the loop so that each trip performs "
"several original iterations. Lowering later converts the result to LLVM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:31
msgid ""
"In this chapter, **MIR transforms** means transforms over cuda-oxide's "
"imported `dialect-mir`. They do not modify rustc's internal MIR directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:37
msgid "Where transforms run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:39
msgid ""
"The optimization layer sits in the middle of the device compilation pipeline:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:41
msgid ""
"Rust source\n"
"    → rustc MIR\n"
"    → mir-importer\n"
"    → dialect-mir\n"
"    → mem2reg\n"
"    → mir-transforms       ← cuda-oxide optimizations\n"
"    → mir-lower\n"
"    → LLVM dialect\n"
"    → LLVM IR\n"
"    → PTX\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:54
msgid ""
"The [MIR importer](mir-importer.md) initially represents local variables "
"with loads and stores. `mem2reg` promotes eligible locals to **SSA values**, "
"where each value has one definition and block arguments carry values between "
"blocks. That makes loop counters and their updates explicit enough for an "
"optimization pass to reason about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:60
msgid ""
"Transforms run here because `dialect-mir` retains Rust-level types and "
"operations, making targeted cuda-oxide rewrites easier to express. The "
"[lowering pipeline](lowering-pipeline.md) then converts the optimized result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:64
msgid ""
"Full variable-debug builds skip `mem2reg` and loop unrolling. Keeping source "
"variables in stable memory locations gives cuda-gdb a better debugging "
"experience."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:68
msgid "Diagnosing locals that remain stack-backed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:70
msgid ""
"An optimized verbose build also checks the exact LLVM IR that will be passed "
"to `llc`. If a Rust local's stack allocation survives both cuda-oxide "
"`mem2reg` and LLVM's `-O2` scalar-replacement pipeline, cuda-oxide reports "
"it before PTX generation. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:75
msgid ""
"warning: local `scratch` (`[u32; 4]`, 16 bytes) in `kernel` could not be "
"promoted to registers\n"
"  = note: indexed by a non-constant value; the allocation survives LLVM "
"scalar replacement and remains stack-backed\n"
"  = help: use constant indexing or a small match/select when the index range "
"is bounded\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:81
msgid ""
"Dynamic indexing is one common blocker because LLVM cannot replace a small "
"aggregate with independent SSA values when the selected element is not "
"known. An address passed to another function, stored as a pointer, returned, "
"or otherwise made observable can also prevent promotion. When the surviving "
"use pattern is not one of the conservative cases cuda-oxide recognizes, the "
"warning still identifies the local but gives a generic scalar-replacement "
"explanation rather than guessing at the cause."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:89
msgid ""
"The check runs only after a successful LLVM optimization pass and only in "
"verbose mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:92
#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:390
msgid "cargo oxide build <example> --verbose\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:96
msgid ""
"This placement matters. Reporting immediately after cuda-oxide `mem2reg` "
"would produce false positives for allocations that LLVM SROA removes later. "
"The check is skipped for no-optimization builds and full variable-debug "
"builds because those modes intentionally retain storage that an optimized "
"build would normally try to promote."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:104
msgid "The first pass: loop unrolling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:106
msgid ""
"A loop normally tests its condition and branches back after every iteration. "
"**Unrolling** copies the body so that one trip through the new loop performs "
"several iterations' work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:110
msgid "Consider this small counted loop inside a kernel or device function:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:112
msgid ""
"let mut i = 0u32;\n"
"#[unroll]\n"
"while i < 4 {\n"
"    work(i);\n"
"    i += 1;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:121
msgid "Conceptually, full unrolling turns it into:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:123
msgid ""
"work(0);\n"
"work(1);\n"
"work(2);\n"
"work(3);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:130
msgid ""
"The condition and branch back to the loop header disappear. More "
"importantly, each copy now has a constant value for `i`, exposing further "
"simplifications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:133
msgid ""
"The tradeoff is larger generated code. A larger body may also need more "
"registers, so unrolling is a request to the compiler, not a promise of "
"better performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:139
msgid "Full and partial unrolling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:141
msgid "cuda-oxide provides two forms:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "Annotation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "Use it when"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "What the pass produces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "`#[unroll]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "The trip count is known at compile time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "One body copy per iteration; the loop disappears"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "`#[unroll(N)]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "The trip count may be known only at runtime"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:30
msgid "`N` copies in a main loop, plus a remainder loop"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:148
msgid "Full unrolling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:150
msgid "Bare `#[unroll]` needs a compile-time-known trip count:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:152
msgid ""
"let mut i = 0u32;\n"
"#[unroll]\n"
"while i < 8 {\n"
"    sum += i;\n"
"    i += 1;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:161
msgid ""
"The pass gives the eight copies counter values `0` through `7`. Constant "
"propagation can then fold expressions derived from those literals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:164
msgid ""
"The bound does not have to be a literal. The pass folds integer arithmetic "
"on constants before it reads the trip count, so `while i < N / 8` unrolls in "
"each instantiation of a function with a const generic `N`. Casts and "
"overflow-checked arithmetic in the bound are not yet folded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:169
msgid "Partial unrolling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:171
msgid "`#[unroll(4)]` also works when the limit is a runtime value:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:173
msgid ""
"let mut i = 0u32;\n"
"#[unroll(4)]\n"
"while i < n {\n"
"    process(i);\n"
"    i += 1;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:182
msgid ""
"If `n` is `10`, the main loop handles two groups of four iterations. The "
"remainder loop handles iterations `8` and `9`. The result is still correct "
"when `n` is not divisible by four."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:186
msgid ""
"This removes three out of every four main-loop tests and gives the compiler "
"a larger straight-line region to optimize."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:191
msgid "A useful peephole: folding pipeline stages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:193
msgid ""
"Partial unrolling has one wrinkle: the main loop still has a runtime "
"counter. cuda-oxide includes a small **peephole optimization** for a common "
"software-pipeline pattern:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:197
msgid ""
"let mut k_idx = 0u32;\n"
"#[unroll(4)]\n"
"while k_idx < k_iters {\n"
"    let stage = k_idx & 3;\n"
"    match stage {\n"
"        0 => consume_stage_0(),\n"
"        1 => consume_stage_1(),\n"
"        2 => consume_stage_2(),\n"
"        3 => consume_stage_3(),\n"
"        _ => {}\n"
"    }\n"
"    k_idx += 1;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:213
msgid ""
"The unrolled main-loop counter advances by four. Its low two bits therefore "
"do not change between groups, so the four copies have fixed stage values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:216
msgid ""
"copy 0: (k_idx + 0) & 3  →  0\n"
"copy 1: (k_idx + 1) & 3  →  1\n"
"copy 2: (k_idx + 2) & 3  →  2\n"
"copy 3: (k_idx + 3) & 3  →  3\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:223
msgid ""
"The peephole replaces those expressions with literals. Constant propagation "
"then removes the dead `match` arms, leaving four straight-line, stage-"
"specific bodies. The remainder loop stays intact for leftover iterations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:228
msgid ""
"`k_idx % 4` works too when `k_idx` is unsigned, and constant offsets such as "
"`(k_idx + 1) & 3` are also recognized. The pass folds these forms only when "
"their power-of-two period divides the unrolled step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:232
msgid "Why a runtime base does not fold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:234
msgid "The expression must be based directly on the loop counter:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:236
msgid ""
"let stage = k_idx & 3;                    // can fold\n"
"\n"
"let global_k = tile_k_base + k_idx;\n"
"let stage = global_k & 3;                 // tile_k_base is runtime: cannot "
"fold\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:243
msgid ""
"Because the low bits of runtime `tile_k_base` are unknown at compile time, "
"the compiler cannot choose one literal stage for each copy. Rewriting it to "
"the first form is correct only when the algorithm separately guarantees that "
"`tile_k_base` is a multiple of four. The pass deliberately refuses to guess."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:249
msgid ""
"Full unrolling does not need this peephole. A fully unrolled counter is "
"already a literal in every copy, so ordinary constant propagation can do the "
"work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:255
msgid "How the annotation reaches the pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:257
msgid "The source attribute travels through the compiler in six stages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:259
msgid ""
"The `#[kernel]` or `#[device]` macro consumes `#[unroll]` or `#[unroll(N)]` "
"and inserts an internal marker call at the start of that loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:261
msgid ""
"`mir-importer` recognizes the call and emits a `mir.unroll_hint` operation. "
"The marker produces no runtime code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:263
msgid ""
"Constant propagation folds constant expressions, such as a bound written "
"`N / 8`. `LoopInfo` then finds the enclosing loop, while induction analysis "
"finds its counter, start, step, bound, and—when possible—trip count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:266
msgid ""
"The pass normalizes supported control flow. It removes branches to blocks "
"that only reach `mir.unreachable`, such as the arm rustc adds to a `match` "
"on an enum for discriminants it cannot have, so they do not count as loop "
"exits. It joins multiple `continue` paths into one path back to the loop "
"header and routes supported loop-carried values out of a fully unrolled loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:272
msgid ""
"The transform clones and reconnects the body for full or partial unrolling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:273
msgid ""
"A cleanup phase folds constants, simplifies branches, and removes dead or "
"unreachable code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:276
msgid ""
"The unroll pass leaves functions without an unroll hint untouched. If it "
"cannot prove a requested rewrite is safe, it prints a warning and does not "
"apply the unroll; the loop continues with the same behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:282
msgid "Supported loops and safe fallback"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:284
msgid ""
"The current pass intentionally recognizes a conservative set of loop shapes. "
"A counted `while` loop and a range `for` loop (`for i in a..b` and "
"`(a..b).rev()`, over any integer counter including `isize`) need a counter "
"the pass can follow: the same step on every path back to the header and a "
"simple, side-effect-free test that directly compares the counter with its "
"bound. Full unrolling also needs a compile-time-constant starting value and "
"step. Partial unrolling accepts a runtime starting value and a step that is "
"a runtime value fixed for the whole loop, such as `blockDim_x()` in a block-"
"stride loop. The loop must also have one ordinary entry before its header. "
"For unusual or irreducible control flow, the compiler warns and does not "
"unroll the loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Shape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Current behavior"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Explicit counted `while` loop"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid ""
"Supported, over any integer counter including `isize`, when the requirements "
"above hold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Range `for` loop (`for i in a..b` and `(a..b).rev()`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Other iterator `for` loops (`a..=b`, `.step_by(k)`, `.iter()`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Not yet recognized; warns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Constant trip count with `#[unroll]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Fully unrolled"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Runtime, loop-invariant limit with `#[unroll(N)]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Partially unrolled"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid ""
"Block-stride loop (`i = threadIdx_x(); while i < n { ..; i += blockDim_x() }"
"`) with `#[unroll(N)]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Several `continue` paths with the same counter step"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Joined and unrolled"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Early `break` or multiple exits with `#[unroll]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Supported"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Early `break` or another exit with `#[unroll(N)]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Warns; partial unroll is skipped"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "A `match` on an enum in the body, such as `opt.unwrap_or(x)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Not an exit; its `unreachable` arm is removed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Nested loops"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:248
msgid "Only the annotated loop is unrolled"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:309
msgid ""
"Partial unrolling also requires `N >= 2`, a `<` or `<=` comparison, and a "
"limit that does not change inside the loop. A compile-time-constant step "
"must be positive. A runtime step, and a limit the loop recomputes on every "
"iteration (such as `n as usize` in `while i < n as usize`), must be computed "
"only from values set before the loop, constants, launch-geometry reads such "
"as `blockDim_x()` or `threadIdx_x()`, integer casts, and `+`, `-`, or `*`. "
"With a runtime step the unrolled loop runs a group of `N` iterations only "
"when the loop's own test passes for every counter value in the group, "
"computed with the same wrapping arithmetic as the loop; otherwise the "
"remaining iterations run one at a time in the original loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:320
msgid ""
"When an annotated outer loop contains an unannotated inner loop, each outer "
"copy receives an intact clone of that inner loop; the inner loop remains a "
"loop. When both loops are annotated, the compiler handles the inner loop "
"first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:325
msgid ""
"To bound compile time and memory, one annotation may create at most 1,024 "
"body copies, 8,192 cloned basic blocks, and 65,536 cloned operations. A "
"request over one of those limits warns and is not unrolled. Every warning "
"about a loop that was not unrolled is an ordinary compiler warning that "
"points at the `#[unroll]` attribute it refers to: rustc prints its "
"`file:line:column` on a `-->` line and underlines it, so a kernel with "
"several annotated loops shows which one was left alone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:333
msgid ""
"For the complete kernel-author reference, see {ref}`Loop unrolling <loop-"
"unrolling>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:337
msgid "When should you unroll?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:339
msgid "Good candidates are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:341
msgid "small loops with a fixed trip count;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:342
msgid "hot counted loops where branch overhead matters;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:343
msgid ""
"software pipelines whose stage selection becomes constant after unrolling;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:344
msgid ""
"loops where several independent copies expose instruction-level parallelism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:346
msgid ""
"Be cautious when the body is large or already uses many registers. Extra "
"code can increase register pressure, reduce occupancy, or put more pressure "
"on the instruction cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:350
msgid ""
"Choose a factor that matches the structure of the algorithm—often a pipeline "
"depth or another small natural group—and benchmark the result. Removing "
"`#[unroll]` should always remain an easy comparison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:356
msgid "Keeping a loop rolled with `#[nounroll]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:358
msgid ""
"The unroll pass only acts on `#[unroll]` requests, but LLVM, libNVVM, and "
"ptxas have their own unrollers. ptxas in particular fully unrolls small "
"constant-trip-count loops even when the PTX keeps them rolled. `#[nounroll]` "
"asks all three to leave a loop alone. It does not go through the unroll pass:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:363
msgid ""
"The macro inserts a different marker, and `mir-importer` emits a "
"`mir.nounroll_hint` operation inside the loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:365
msgid ""
"The hint survives MIR preparation. The unroll pass ignores it, so unrolling "
"an enclosing loop copies the hint along with the inner loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:367
msgid ""
"Just before lowering, after the last transform that can reshape loops, each "
"hint is mapped to its innermost loop. The loop's back-edges are joined "
"through one latch block, and the hint is removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:370
msgid ""
"Lowering marks that latch's branch, and the LLVM IR exporter attaches `!"
"llvm.loop` metadata with `llvm.loop.unroll.disable` to it. The NVPTX backend "
"prints `.pragma \"nounroll\";` at the loop header; libNVVM reads the same "
"metadata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:375
msgid ""
"Step 3 also runs in full-debug builds, which skip MIR preparation. A hint "
"that is not inside any loop is dropped with a warning that names the "
"attribute's `file:line:column`. A hint in unreachable code is dropped "
"silently. WGMMA counted-loop fusion replaces a K loop with one operation "
"that generates its own PTX loop. It moves a `#[nounroll]` request onto that "
"operation, whose loop header then starts with `.pragma \"nounroll\";`, and "
"leaves the original blocks unreachable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:383
msgid ""
"Like `#[unroll]`, the hint is mapped to the innermost loop around it. A loop "
"whose body never repeats, such as `loop { ...; break; }`, produces no loop "
"in MIR. If it sits inside another loop, the request applies to that "
"enclosing loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:388
msgid "To inspect the pass's analysis, build verbosely:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:394
msgid ""
"Verbose mode prints the requested factor and the loop facts the pass found. "
"If a request is skipped, a warning explains why. Loop numbers and analysis "
"fields are internal details and may change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:400
msgid "A home for future transforms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:402
msgid ""
"The `mir-transforms` crate keeps reusable analysis separate from IR mutation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:404
msgid ""
"cuda-oxide/crates/mir-transforms/src/\n"
"├── analyses/\n"
"│   ├── loop_info.rs      # which blocks belong to each loop?\n"
"│   └── induction.rs      # what are the counter, step, bound, and trip "
"count?\n"
"├── canonicalize.rs       # put supported control flow into a simpler shape\n"
"└── unroll.rs             # prove, clone, reconnect, and clean up loops\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:413
msgid ""
"This separation gives future optimizations a predictable place to live: "
"analyses gather facts, canonicalization normalizes equivalent shapes, and "
"transforms perform behavior-preserving rewrites. `mir-importer/src/"
"pipeline.rs` controls when each pass runs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:418
msgid ""
"Every transform should follow the same rule as the unroller: prove the "
"rewrite is safe, bound its resource use, and fall back without changing "
"behavior when a program is outside the supported shape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../compiler/compiler-optimizations.md:422
msgid "The focused test suite runs with:"
